Word-boundary and punctuation-attached i get large predictions; a word-internal
letter gets less. The loop/declaration words int, for and while boost spaced i.
Common words and distinctive accented letters estimate language, reducing i in
contexts where it is an ordinary foreign function word. Smaller rules cover
repeated i, j/e, Turkish and Ukrainian i forms, and Arabic alef. All other tokens
get zero: weak vocabulary-specific responses are deliberately omitted.

Keep only the preceding 300 characters, resetting at document boundaries.
"""
import re

READS = ()
LANGUAGE_WORDS = {
    'Italian': frozenset('di che il gli dei del della delle degli per una sono '
                         'non con alla nella tutti più anche questo questa come '
                         'ma era da senza tra fra nostro nostri può essere lo '
                         'le un e'.split()),
    'Polish': frozenset('się nie jest to na że do oraz ale jak czy tego przez '
                        'dla przy tym był była które jego będzie tak'.split()),
    'Scandinavian': frozenset('og och att det är har en et av som på med til '
                             'den om vi jeg jag kan ikke var han från der så'.split()),
    'Czech': frozenset('že se na je jsou pro ale jako do v při nebo tak '
                       'jeho od s'.split()),
    'English': frozenset('the a an to of and is are that this in was it with '
                         'for from have has be by but not will can you we '
                         'they my your as at'.split()),
}


def language(text):
    lower = text.lower()
    words = re.findall(r'[^\W\d_]+', lower)
    scores = {name: sum(word in lexicon for word in words)
              for name, lexicon in LANGUAGE_WORDS.items()}
